# Who gets asked when something changes.
#
# Review is not *required* — the ruleset sets `require_code_owner_review: false`
# and zero required approvals, because there is one developer and a rule nobody
# can satisfy is a rule that gets bypassed. What this file does is put a review
# request in front of a human automatically.
#
# There is deliberately no `*` rule. A default that names the same two people as
# every specific rule makes the specific rules decorative and requests review on
# every pull request regardless of content — and undifferentiated requests get
# muted, which costs exactly the paths below the attention they are listed for.
# Only changes that touch something expensive ask for a second pair of eyes.

# The access model. A mistake here is not a bug in a feature — it is who can
# read whose medical records. The chain owns the schema, the three roles and,
# from M2, every RLS policy.
/api/migrations/                            @SimonOsipov @porollo-dev

# The impersonation seam and the token check. Everything above rests on these
# two: the seam is what makes a request run as the caller, the verifier is what
# decides who the caller is.
